1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:15,588
AI security incidents have exploded over the last couple of months, and honestly, at this point, it really feels like there is a new one every single week, but feelings don't always match reality, so I thought I'd look at the actual data, and indeed that feeling is accurate this time.

2
00:00:16,110 --> 00:00:26,320
In October alone, Microsoft fixed more than 1,400 security flaws, a record for them, and the number of flaws attackers are actually using has nearly doubled this year.

3
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:28,004
So why is this happening?

4
00:00:28,622 --> 00:00:32,196
While AI has become much better at finding security vulnerabilities.

5
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:44,560
Earlier this month, researchers used AI to help uncover two serious flaws on PHP, and within hours of the flaws being publicly disclosed, attackers had launched millions of attempts to exploit them.

6
00:00:44,878 --> 00:00:47,236
These people wasted absolutely no time.

7
00:00:47,758 --> 00:00:59,360
The biggest security story this week, however, sadly, was the leak of records on 41,000 homeland security staff, and as far as anyone has reported so far, no AI was involved at all.

8
00:00:59,598 --> 00:01:02,532
So let's just be honest about something at the start of this video.

9
00:01:02,862 --> 00:01:09,360
Hacking has been around since the dawn of computing, and there will always be bad actors interested in this sort of thing.

10
00:01:09,710 --> 00:01:14,820
What AI has done though is create yet another unique environment where it can happen.

11
00:01:15,150 --> 00:01:23,556
It opens up new ways in, and it's very good at finding them, which is a very big part of why the number of flaws being found has exploded so rapidly.

12
00:01:23,982 --> 00:01:40,480
It's also interesting that as these giant AI companies get more and more interested in going public, like Anthropic, which filed for its IPO earlier this week, so much of the conversation turns to demonstrating what AI can do, and they keep reaching for very interesting ways to demonstrate this.

13
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:46,628
Sometimes it's human extinction, sometimes it's Sam Altman saying we can't patch all the bugs anymore.

14
00:01:46,894 --> 00:01:47,396
That works.

15
00:01:47,950 --> 00:02:01,520
But instead of going with the hype that everyone's going to die or get hacked or that the internet is going to collapse underneath us, I think a much more prudent, scientific, and balanced approach is to see the situation for what it actually is.

16
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:10,080
Look at the reality, look at what the research is actually saying, and just try to understand how the whole ecosystem around AI has to evolve.

17
00:02:10,350 --> 00:02:14,596
So in this video, I want to start with the security flaws AI is finding.

18
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:22,052
Then, if AI is that good at finding flaws, we have to examine whether AI can actually fix them, fight fire with fire, right?

19
00:02:22,638 --> 00:02:32,932
Then we need to go hardcore and look at what the research actually says about how far along researchers are and what it would actually take for the whole ecosystem around AI to catch up.

20
00:02:33,358 --> 00:02:43,940
And finally, equally importantly, we need to discuss the money holding all of this together, because these companies are trying to IPO in an era where these incidents continue to stack up.

21
00:02:44,430 --> 00:02:52,800
So let's start with the flaws themselves, because if AI really is finding more of them, the obvious question is whether that actually matters.

22
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:56,900
The world has plenty of little bugs lying around that are totally inconsequential.

23
00:02:57,422 --> 00:03:01,860
Well, the best way to answer this is with the flaw that shook up the internet last week.

24
00:03:02,318 --> 00:03:03,840
You've probably heard of it by now.

25
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:11,556
It's called PHP to Shell, and the PHP part is the programming language running behind roughly seven in ten websites, so quite a lot.

26
00:03:12,110 --> 00:03:16,836
But what got a bit lost in all of the noise is how long it had actually been there.

27
00:03:17,326 --> 00:03:25,284
According to Tenable's Gavin Miller, the bugs had been sitting in that code for more than a decade, and in all of that time, nobody had spotted them.

28
00:03:25,646 --> 00:03:29,520
Kind of like that spider that is probably somewhere under your bed that you don't know about.

29
00:03:29,710 --> 00:03:33,840
And yes, that is the last Halloween reference I'll make in this video, or is it?

30
00:03:34,446 --> 00:03:52,036
In August, a security firm called Caliph found these bugs for the first time using one of OpenAI's models as part of an OpenAI program called Patch the Planet, and another firm, Wiz, found the same bugs independently, also with help from AI Tools, so you know this is legit.

31
00:03:52,590 --> 00:03:59,588
So basically, a flaw nobody had noticed for over 10 years got found twice within just a span of few weeks.

32
00:04:00,110 --> 00:04:08,804
And that's really the shift AI brings, because once finding the flaws gets this easy, you cannot assume the good guys are the only ones looking, right?

33
00:04:09,326 --> 00:04:26,244
What's less comforting is that somebody else already knew about these bugs too, because just four days before the fix even came out, Amazon caught people already trying to use the flaw on its honey pots, which are basically just computers left out of spade so you can watch who tries to break in.

34
00:04:26,704 --> 00:04:31,984
Then, once the fix came out, the millions of attempts you heard about earlier started pouring in.

35
00:04:32,144 --> 00:04:51,812
At first, most of them failed because no public attack worked reliably on a normal PHP setup, but within five days, a researcher confirmed attackers were successfully getting in, so even with a fix out there, anyone who hadn't installed it yet was basically a sitting duck, and that's really the heart of this problem.

36
00:04:52,270 --> 00:05:04,868
With AI finding the flaw has become the quick part, while getting millions of websites to actually install the fix overnight is still the very slow part, and the danger lives in the gap between these two.

37
00:05:05,550 --> 00:05:13,572
If PHP to Shell were a one-off, it would just be a scary Halloween-y story, but the same thing is sadly happening everywhere.

38
00:05:13,902 --> 00:05:17,664
Remember Microsoft's record month that I mentioned earlier at the start of this video?

39
00:05:17,824 --> 00:05:24,784
Well, they actually credit AI models for a lot of that number, and at least one of them was already being used by attackers.

40
00:05:24,864 --> 00:05:32,676
So even Microsoft, with the fleet of resources that it has, was fixing some of these after attackers had already got there.

41
00:05:32,942 --> 00:05:47,172
And across the whole industry, I'm telling you, Google found that the number of flaws published each month more than doubled this year, my friends, from about 5,000 in January, the olden days of January, to more than 10,700 in August.

42
00:05:47,822 --> 00:05:51,492
Now, to be fair, most of the flood has a silver lining, I guess.

43
00:05:51,918 --> 00:05:57,124
A flaw usually only gets published once somebody has found it and a fix is ready.

44
00:05:57,390 --> 00:06:07,556
And Google saw only about 1 in 431 of this year's flaws actually being used by attackers, so the sheer number isn't necessarily the scary part on its own.

45
00:06:07,886 --> 00:06:13,184
What matters is which flaws attackers go after and how fast they get there.

46
00:06:13,344 --> 00:06:14,500
That's the critical bit.

47
00:06:14,798 --> 00:06:17,092
And sadly, this is where it gets even worse.

48
00:06:17,344 --> 00:06:25,252
The most dangerous kind of flaw is the one that lets an attacker run their own code on your computer, and that's exactly what PHP To Shell did.

49
00:06:25,678 --> 00:06:27,424
And Google actually looked into this.

50
00:06:27,630 --> 00:06:31,876
Of the flaws it thinks were found with AI, half were this dangerous kind.

51
00:06:32,064 --> 00:06:34,052
But for the others, it was just about a quarter.

52
00:06:34,510 --> 00:06:40,292
And PHP to shell showed us something else very interesting as well, that attackers move very quickly.

53
00:06:40,814 --> 00:06:46,980
Some of them had the flaw before a fix even existed, and others were breaking in just within five days.

54
00:06:47,918 --> 00:06:56,304
So if finding flaws is the quick part now, and fixing them is the slow part, why not just get AI to do the fixing as well and just fight fire with fire?

55
00:06:56,654 --> 00:07:00,516
So it's like, okay, there are these models, they can find a bunch of security vulnerabilities.

56
00:07:00,942 --> 00:07:06,980
Those are probably all of the security vulnerabilities that exist, or all the security vulnerabilities that models are ever going to find.

57
00:07:07,630 --> 00:07:12,304
And I don't think most people sort of said, okay, the models are going to get much smarter.

58
00:07:12,384 --> 00:07:21,572
They can probably find bugs, like crazy bugs, you know, bugs that are outside of the normal threat model of what we think a hacker can do.

59
00:07:22,064 --> 00:07:24,868
Um, you know, hardware level, insane stuff, whatever.

60
00:07:25,584 --> 00:07:28,932
And so people fix some bugs.

61
00:07:29,838 --> 00:07:36,624
And, you know, security professionals still freaking out, CISO's still freaking out, but and I I no longer think we're going to be able to just patch all the bugs.

62
00:07:36,704 --> 00:07:44,004
I think we're gonna have to completely switch the defense model to sort of using the same technology to for sort of a new kind of defense shield.

63
00:07:46,510 --> 00:07:48,944
And I think it's a huge safety and security issue.

64
00:07:49,326 --> 00:07:50,624
Oh wow, how convenient.

65
00:07:50,784 --> 00:07:53,264
You made the problem and now you're selling the fix as well.

66
00:07:53,550 --> 00:07:56,304
Hang on, I've actually got like a trillion dollars lying around somewhere.

67
00:07:56,464 --> 00:07:57,412
Is that enough for the fix?

68
00:07:57,584 --> 00:07:58,564
Thank you so much.

69
00:07:59,182 --> 00:08:01,700
But jokes aside, it is actually a fair question.

70
00:08:02,062 --> 00:08:09,252
If AI can fix flaws as fast as it finds them, the gap between the quick part and the slow part sort of closes.

71
00:08:09,742 --> 00:08:12,064
But only if you can trust the fix itself.

72
00:08:12,334 --> 00:08:15,908
So the question is really whether anyone can check the AI's work.

73
00:08:16,654 --> 00:08:20,612
This month, a team at Northwestern University looked at exactly that.

74
00:08:20,784 --> 00:08:26,724
Most of the scores you see, like AI fix this many bugs, yay, are measured in a pretty simple way.

75
00:08:27,086 --> 00:08:35,108
They take whatever made a program crash, run it again after the AI's fix, and if it doesn't crash anymore, that counts as a success.

76
00:08:35,790 --> 00:08:38,544
But a bug can be a lot bigger than just one crash.

77
00:08:38,624 --> 00:08:43,684
It's a little bit like a mechanic who gets rid of the rattle in your car by taking out the part that rattles.

78
00:08:44,046 --> 00:08:47,588
The noise is gone, sure, but now the car doesn't really work properly.

79
00:08:48,046 --> 00:08:56,868
So the researchers check the AI's fixes against the tests the project's own developers had written, the ones that actually make sure the program still does the job.

80
00:08:57,646 --> 00:09:07,076
Claude Opus 4.7 stopped the crash about 90% of the time, but only about 61% of its fixes passed those tests that I was talking about.

81
00:09:07,470 --> 00:09:12,260
So roughly one in three fixes that looked fine actually kind of broke something.

82
00:09:12,718 --> 00:09:14,944
The models are improving fast, to be fair.

83
00:09:15,104 --> 00:09:21,956
An older cloud model only managed about 44%, and this was just a small study that only used cloud models.

84
00:09:22,304 --> 00:09:35,620
But for now, every AI fix still needs a human to check it, or maybe a much more powerful AI built with security in mind, but regardless, somebody still has to do the slow part, is just checking now rather than fixing.

85
00:09:36,302 --> 00:09:39,024
Some of the fixes weren't even the AI's own work.

86
00:09:39,184 --> 00:09:44,452
Just a quick reminder: agents are AI models that go off and do tasks on their own autonomously.

87
00:09:44,910 --> 00:09:57,412
Some of them basically, instead of fixing things, just searched the internet, found the fix the actual real human developers had already published and just copied it, and the scoreboard still counted that as the AI success.

88
00:09:58,030 --> 00:10:03,584
That happened because none of the testing setups the researchers looked at cut the AI off from the internet.

89
00:10:03,824 --> 00:10:05,732
We've discussed this on this channel before.

90
00:10:06,190 --> 00:10:11,504
So while these models are being tested, nobody is really watching what else they get up to out there.

91
00:10:11,662 --> 00:10:24,068
And it's a big all cluster f this is a huge problem for Altman's Shield that he was talking about, because to fix flaws, you literally have to let the AI loose on actual real systems out there in the world.

92
00:10:24,462 --> 00:10:26,624
Which of course brings us a different question.

93
00:10:26,864 --> 00:10:30,464
Can these companies actually see what their AIs do out there?

94
00:10:30,784 --> 00:10:46,148
well, I'm not really sure that they can a lot of the time, because just as an example, Anthropic was testing its small model, Claude Haiku, on random web pages, and during that test, it submitted a made-up eyewitness tip to a Philadelphia website for unsolved murders.

95
00:10:46,670 --> 00:10:52,740
Weird and a bit random, I know, but thankfully the tip got flagged as spam and never actually reached investigators.

96
00:10:53,294 --> 00:11:01,024
But Anthropic only found this out 72 days later, and Philadelphia police, rightfully in my view, called the delay unacceptable.

97
00:11:01,358 --> 00:11:03,588
The tip itself thankfully did no harm.

98
00:11:03,950 --> 00:11:13,604
What bothers me here is that an AI was out on the open internet doing something nobody asked it to do, and for over two months, nobody at the company noticed.

99
00:11:14,094 --> 00:11:16,544
The companies are starting to admit this as well.

100
00:11:16,704 --> 00:11:26,948
I mean, just last month, one of OpenAI's research models got onto the live internet during training, so now OpenAI trains its most powerful models with no internet access at all.

101
00:11:27,438 --> 00:11:33,508
I guess keeping a computer off the internet isn't exactly new technology, but I guess it's good to see it catching on.

102
00:11:33,966 --> 00:11:38,944
But we can't keep AI unplugged forever, especially if we wanted guarding our systems, right?

103
00:11:39,118 --> 00:11:44,996
So how close are researchers to letting AI defend our systems without it becoming the next way in?

104
00:11:45,390 --> 00:11:51,504
This is where we need to get into the hardcore science that PhD has uses sometimes, and I read the paper so you don't have to.

105
00:11:51,598 --> 00:11:54,980
We'll get into all of that right after a quick word from today's video sponsor.

106
00:11:55,374 --> 00:12:06,756
Klein Desktop is a free open source application that lets you work with AI models across coding, research, document analysis, and other workflows without being locked into a single model provider.

107
00:12:07,246 --> 00:12:13,700
I wanted to see how useful that flexibility actually was, so I gave Klein two very different tasks in parallel.

108
00:12:14,254 --> 00:12:19,876
First, I asked it to analyze documents about a fictional $40 billion AI infrastructure project.

109
