At the same time, I launched another agent to build an interactive financial dashboard from a dataset complete with charts, filters, and investment comparisons.

110
00:12:29,230 --> 00:12:31,136
While they're running, let me show you something else.

111
00:12:31,296 --> 00:12:37,696
You can schedule recurring tasks and extend client through its marketplace with skills, MCP servers, and plugins.

112
00:12:37,966 --> 00:12:44,164
And the first agent is done, it produced a research briefing identifying inconsistencies in the financial claims.

113
00:12:44,526 --> 00:12:49,092
The second one actually one shot at this interactive dashboard, which I can further customize.

114
00:12:49,678 --> 00:13:01,476
For both tasks, I use ClientPass, which is a $9.99 per month subscription, and it provides access to a curated selection of open weight models, including Deepsea, Kimi, and GLM.

115
00:13:01,710 --> 00:13:10,916
Apart from Client Pass, Desktop also supports over 300 models through Client, more than 50 providers through your own API keys, local models, and free models.

116
00:13:11,214 --> 00:13:14,496
If you like to try Client Desktop, it's completely free and open source.

117
00:13:14,576 --> 00:13:16,196
Just use the link in the description below.

118
00:13:16,654 --> 00:13:19,536
Right, so before the break, I asked a pretty big question.

119
00:13:19,758 --> 00:13:26,916
If we're going to let AI defend our systems, how close are researchers to making sure it doesn't become the next way in?

120
00:13:27,470 --> 00:13:30,256
I went through the research from the last few months to find out.

121
00:13:30,416 --> 00:13:39,972
Again, sorry, but I can give you fancy graphics or things moving across your screen every few seconds, but I can give you a sound analysis of where the research is, so just bear with me.

122
00:13:40,526 --> 00:13:48,132
A lot of these papers are in Archive X, which is where researchers post their papers before they've been peer-reviewed, so it's as fresh as it gets.

123
00:13:48,686 --> 00:13:54,820
What surprised me, however, is how often completely different groups keep running into the same few problems.

124
00:13:55,662 --> 00:13:59,684
The first one is that checking the AI's work has become the hard part.

125
00:14:00,014 --> 00:14:09,860
You've already seen this with the Northwestern study that I discussed earlier, where about a third of the fixes that looked fine actually broke something, and they're not the only ones finding this.

126
00:14:10,318 --> 00:14:20,964
Over the summer, 1Password's research group of ByOne Labs graded more than 6,000 security fixes written by AI and found that three in four left something broken.

127
00:14:21,390 --> 00:14:34,212
Even Google, which now automatically attaches AI written fixes to bug reports, admitted that the people who look after open source code are drowning in them and that a lot of them cost more time to review than they're actually worth.

128
00:14:34,656 --> 00:14:36,336
Encryption has the same problem.

129
00:14:36,526 --> 00:14:41,604
For the cool people in this audience, that's the maths that keeps your messages and bank details private.

130
00:14:41,998 --> 00:14:52,976
In July, Anthropic, who also do a lot of research, said its most powerful model had found new weaknesses in some encryption methods, including a weakened version of one of the most widely used ones.

131
00:14:53,326 --> 00:15:02,656
If you're worried about your bank account after that, by the way, you don't have to be, because the full strength versions and the new standards built to survive future quantum computers still hold.

132
00:15:02,830 --> 00:15:03,300
We're good.

133
00:15:03,822 --> 00:15:11,876
The Cloud Security Alliance, which is an industry group, published a research note on this just this month, and their worry, however, is different.

134
00:15:12,366 --> 00:15:19,652
AI can now suggest possible attacks faster than the small number of experts on this planet can actually check them.

135
00:15:20,014 --> 00:15:26,496
So a real problem could sit there unnoticed while everyone's just busy checking the ones that turn out to be nothing.

136
00:15:26,670 --> 00:15:28,036
This is a huge deal, right?

137
00:15:28,526 --> 00:15:31,940
The cryptographer Matthew Green sees it completely the other way.

138
00:15:32,302 --> 00:15:39,136
He thinks the timing is great because everyone is switching to new encryption anyway, so better to find the weak spots now than later.

139
00:15:39,438 --> 00:15:40,932
Personally, I kind of agree with him.

140
00:15:41,518 --> 00:15:44,932
So on checking, researchers are not very close at all.

141
00:15:45,294 --> 00:15:50,244
And it's the same gap that we saw with the attackers earlier, just on the defender side this time.

142
00:15:50,574 --> 00:15:57,056
If you cannot check everything the AI does, the next best thing, I guess, is to limit what it can do in the first place.

143
00:15:57,216 --> 00:16:05,284
And that is exactly the second thing researchers keep coming back to fencing the AI in, and it starts with sadly some bad news.

144
00:16:05,902 --> 00:16:09,060
Part of the problem is something called prompt injection.

145
00:16:09,518 --> 00:16:16,996
That's when somebody hides instructions on a web page or in a document, and an AI that reads it follows them as if they came from you.

146
00:16:17,422 --> 00:16:29,252
In May, a couple of excellent researchers whose names I sadly cannot pronounce tackle this by borrowing an idea from privacy research, which asks whether information is flowing where it's actually supposed to.

147
00:16:29,582 --> 00:16:33,984
They built various scenarios to test it, and what they found was pretty sobering.

148
00:16:34,478 --> 00:16:39,300
An attack can always build a situation where the bad request looks perfectly reasonable.

149
00:16:39,598 --> 00:16:45,824
And if you tighten the rules enough to stop that, you kind of start blocking things the AI should be allowed to do.

150
00:16:46,158 --> 00:16:49,764
So you probably cannot train this problem out of the AI completely.

151
00:16:50,382 --> 00:16:52,612
That's for the bad news, and now good news.

152
00:16:53,134 --> 00:17:00,452
In June, Pranit Narissetti and three colleagues looked at five recent defenses that don't rely on the AI behaving itself at all.

153
00:17:00,624 --> 00:17:08,900
They sit outside the AI and enforce the rules around it, kind of like a bouncer who doesn't care how convincing your story is in front of the club, right?

154
00:17:09,326 --> 00:17:18,384
The team basically reran the attacks against one of those defenses themselves, and it cut the attacker's success rate from about 26% to about 4%.

155
00:17:18,766 --> 00:17:20,228
So that is a significant drop.

156
00:17:20,686 --> 00:17:27,332
Even an attack designed specifically to beat this only got through about 2.6% of the time.

157
00:17:27,854 --> 00:17:34,224
The authors point out in this paper that this was a small test on a fairly small model, but I'm counting it as a win.

158
00:17:34,414 --> 00:17:35,556
It's still a promising finding.

159
00:17:36,206 --> 00:17:41,924
Researchers looking at AI escaping altogether land in a similar place, if not the same place.

160
00:17:42,382 --> 00:17:49,764
OpenAI has said that in its own tests, its agents chain flaws together to break out of the lockdown machines they were running on.

161
00:17:50,286 --> 00:18:00,804
Two weeks ago, Alicia Colmonera Fernandez recreated that setup on her own machines and reproduced the weakness with nothing more exotic than basic web requests and a bit of Python.

162
00:18:01,134 --> 00:18:03,588
She published her code, so anyone can repeat this.

163
00:18:03,982 --> 00:18:14,180
Her conclusion is that a standard security approach called zero trust, which basically just means never automatically trusting anything, even inside your own network, would have shut this down.

164
00:18:14,766 --> 00:18:25,476
Around the same time, Abbas Raftari compared this year's incidents where agents from OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google reach real systems outside the area they were meant to be tested in.

165
00:18:25,966 --> 00:18:33,124
He worked from the company's public reports, and he treats the Google case as provisional because there's less on the record on that one.

166
00:18:33,614 --> 00:18:40,420
Then he laid out what a proper fence would actually look like in five layers, and three of them are worth knowing, so we're going to discuss them.

167
00:18:41,038 --> 00:18:49,124
First, the internet is switched off by default, and that's enforced from outside the AI's own machine, so it cannot just switch it back on again.

168
00:18:49,870 --> 00:18:55,812
Second, any passwords or keys the AI gets only work for that one job and run out quickly.

169
00:18:56,494 --> 00:19:01,444
Third, somebody keeps watch on what the AI does, not just the reasoning it writes down.

170
00:19:01,966 --> 00:19:11,556
There is also another very interesting paper that I'm going to link down in the description as well in the sources by Abu Bakr Siddhik at Rashani University of Engineering and Technology in Bangladesh.

171
00:19:11,886 --> 00:19:16,784
They reviewed 97 sources over the summer and essentially came to the following conclusion.

172
00:19:17,038 --> 00:19:20,464
The test setup itself is part of the security.

173
00:19:20,624 --> 00:19:21,572
This is the key bit.

174
00:19:21,970 --> 00:19:24,864
All right, let's watch this video of a baby otter to relax for a little bit.

175
00:19:25,024 --> 00:19:28,384
It has nothing to do with AI security, and that's exactly the point.

176
00:19:28,846 --> 00:19:29,508
Cutie, right?

177
00:19:29,998 --> 00:19:38,704
Anyway, on fencing AI in, researchers are actually a lot closer than the headlines suggest, because none of this needs a scientific breakthrough.

178
00:19:38,864 --> 00:19:42,532
These are tools we already have at our disposal and we can just use them.

179
00:19:42,830 --> 00:19:47,012
They are just now standard yet, and that has worried me for many videos already.

180
00:19:47,470 --> 00:19:50,704
But a fence only works if you know what is inside it.

181
00:19:50,784 --> 00:19:56,784
That is the third problem researchers keep running into, and the one they're furthest from solving, in my opinion.

182
00:19:57,024 --> 00:19:59,460
It's also the oldest problem in security.

183
00:19:59,854 --> 00:20:10,020
When an AI agent logs into a system, changes something, or sends a message, you need to know which agent it was, who it belongs to, and what it's allowed to do.

184
00:20:10,690 --> 00:20:19,908
America's Standards Body Nist asked the industry how to do that and got more than 600 responses, and it still hasn't released its rules for AA agents.

185
00:20:20,526 --> 00:20:30,752
Meanwhile, several groups have put forward their own systems, and two of them have even ended up with the same name, which basically tells you roughly how settled this whole shenanigan is.

186
00:20:31,086 --> 00:20:36,676
And remember that Homeland Security leak from the start with the 41,000 people whose personal data got exposed?

187
00:20:37,038 --> 00:20:47,044
The people who published it say it came through the department's login system, that's the part that checks who you are, although the company that runs it says its systems were not breached.

188
00:20:47,470 --> 00:20:56,100
Either way, if we still struggle to keep track of which people should be let in, keeping track of thousands of AI agents is a much bigger job, right?

189
00:20:56,654 --> 00:21:02,404
So how close are researchers to letting AI defend our systems without it becoming the next way in?

190
00:21:02,862 --> 00:21:09,284
Almost all of this research, my friends, is brand new and hasn't quite been peer-reviewed yet, so it will keep moving.

191
00:21:09,678 --> 00:21:17,988
But right now, they're actually closer than you'd think on fencing AI in and a long way off on checking its work and knowing which AI did what.

192
00:21:18,574 --> 00:21:27,492
And in all of these, the fixes are in the systems around the AI, where it runs, how it's tested, and who's watching it, those haven't been built to keep up yet.

193
00:21:28,078 --> 00:21:33,956
And yet the companies building this AI want the public's money right now before any of that is built.

194
00:21:34,638 --> 00:21:39,332
So what happens when they try to raise it while the incidents keep stacking up like this, huh?

195
00:21:39,632 --> 00:21:44,324
Well, this week we got our first real look because Anthropic filed to go public.

196
00:21:44,686 --> 00:21:52,192
Before a company can sell shares on the stock market, it has to publish a long document telling investors everything that could actually go wrong.

197
00:21:52,526 --> 00:21:55,332
And Anthropics has more than 90 pages of risks.

198
00:21:55,552 --> 00:21:57,152
I mean it's risks galore.

199
00:21:57,232 --> 00:22:01,988
They should have literally just opened the prospectus with trick-or-treat, give us money or we mess you up.

200
00:22:02,352 --> 00:22:13,028
Reading the prospectus after everything we've discussed in this video is a bit of a strange experience because a lot of what we discussed in this video is hinted at in the prospectus minus the literature study.

201
00:22:13,422 --> 00:22:17,220
Anthropic says each version of Claude helps build the next one.

202
00:22:17,646 --> 00:22:31,044
They warn that flaws could pile up in ways that may be difficult to detect or remediate, which is a very formal way of describing a model sending in a fake unalibing tip that nobody at the company noticed for 72 days, I'm just saying.

203
00:22:31,502 --> 00:22:36,484
And it says bad actors have already used AI to carry out serious cyber attacks.

204
00:22:37,006 --> 00:22:44,516
So the flaws, the checking problem, and the missing fence are all now written into a legal document that's being used to sell shares.

205
00:22:44,974 --> 00:22:46,340
Who wouldn't want to buy that, right?

206
00:22:46,862 --> 00:22:49,152
Naturally, other people have noticed as well.

207
00:22:49,312 --> 00:22:54,212
Just this month, Mark Levine wrote to Anthropic and urged it to pause the IPO.

208
00:22:54,606 --> 00:23:02,352
That guy oversees New York City's pension system, which is $330 billion, covering more than 750,000 people.

209
00:23:02,638 --> 00:23:04,804
This is a serious operation, it's not a joke.

210
00:23:05,262 --> 00:23:12,272
His concerns were primarily safety, how the company is run and overseen, and how fast all of this is actually moving.

211
00:23:12,622 --> 00:23:20,708
So the same risks Anthropic listed to sell the shares are now why one of America's big pension funds wants the sale put on hold.

212
00:23:21,102 --> 00:23:26,432
And the pension system only has about $7 million in Anthropic, by the way, which is tiny for them.

213
00:23:26,830 --> 00:23:32,432
Levine's worry is basically whether anyone should be buying in before the problems are sorted.

214
00:23:32,512 --> 00:23:35,236
And I think this is a very valuable question to sit with.

215
00:23:35,822 --> 00:23:48,292
Now, to be fair to Anthropic, companies have to warn investors about every serious thing that could go wrong, so a long list of scary risks is a pretty reasonable and normal way that a filing like this can take shape.

216
00:23:48,750 --> 00:24:02,352
Still, of course, plenty of people argue that all the talk about how dangerous AI is mostly makes it sound more powerful and therefore perhaps more valuable, but warning or sales pitch, it doesn't change what we've actually seen in this video.

217
00:24:02,638 --> 00:24:09,232
The flaws are real, the fixes still need checking, and nobody has finished the fencing around AI.

218
00:24:09,392 --> 00:24:11,872
And that matters more than usual on this occasion.

219
00:24:12,032 --> 00:24:18,980
As I went through in my last video, a lot of the money behind these companies goes around in circles between the same few giants.

220
00:24:19,310 --> 00:24:23,232
They invest in each other, lend to each other, and sell each other computing power.

221
00:24:23,566 --> 00:24:31,072
When money is tied together like that, a serious security incident at one company doesn't necessarily stay at that company.

222
00:24:31,246 --> 00:24:40,112
So if you're thinking of buying in, you're also betting that the whole world around AI can keep up with the risks these companies are now openly admitting to.

223
00:24:40,366 --> 00:24:45,412
Again, I'm not giving any financial advice, I'm just thinking of things that we definitely should be focusing on.

224
00:24:46,030 --> 00:24:49,060
So, in light of all of this, where does all of this leave us?

225
00:24:49,806 --> 00:24:53,328
Like I've said before, AI is a big shift and it is happening.

226
00:24:53,488 --> 00:25:01,636
We can keep kicking and screaming about it, but it is going to happen, it's happening already, and the best thing, in my view, that we can do is just evolve with it.

227
00:25:02,094 --> 00:25:11,012
That might be how we think about it or how we use it, but it's definitely the software we build, the security standards, the algorithms, and the architecture.

228
00:25:11,406 --> 00:25:18,564
A lot of the infrastructure we have today has to grow up alongside this technology, as I've said multiple times on this channel before.

229
00:25:18,926 --> 00:25:21,220
The companies are starting to get there as well.

230
00:25:21,582 --> 00:25:26,048
Even OpenAI now keeps its most powerful models off the internet while it trains them.

231
00:25:26,128 --> 00:25:29,028
So that is definitely a very good step in the right direction.

232
00:25:29,582 --> 00:25:33,248
And I have to say another thing here: a lot of people think that I'm anti-AI.

233
00:25:33,328 --> 00:25:35,488
So let me just clarify this once and for all.

234
00:25:35,648 --> 00:25:38,848
I am one of the least anti-AI people you're ever going to meet.

235
00:25:38,928 --> 00:25:40,804
I am vehemently pro-AI.

236
00:25:41,166 --> 00:25:42,448
I love this technology.

237
00:25:42,608 --> 00:25:46,820
I use it for all sorts of things, and I encourage other people to use it as well.

238
00:25:47,310 --> 00:25:50,692
But that doesn't mean that I'm going to buy everything these companies say.

239
00:25:51,118 --> 00:25:57,252
And just so you know where I stand here, I don't have all of the answers about which architecture is the right one here.

240
00:25:57,582 --> 00:26:00,448
I'm not a cryptographer and I'm not a security expert.

241
00:26:00,750 --> 00:26:09,988
I'm a computer scientist who took a cryptography course in my undergrad, so I know the basics, but I am by far not one of the security researchers who should be weighing in on this one.

242
00:26:10,254 --> 00:26:14,372
I'm really grateful to all of the researchers doing this work, but I'm not one of them.

243
00:26:14,862 --> 00:26:18,532
How we handle AI, however, is literally on all of us.

244
00:26:19,086 --> 00:26:30,116
Sure, most of us don't have a trillion dollars lying around or companies buying GPUs by the dozen, but we can think critically, be curious about the evidence, read the research and not bind the hype.

245
00:26:30,574 --> 00:26:39,652
Whether it's AI is going to kill us all, AI is going to hack us all, or hell, even AI is going to give us a utopia, if it's extreme, my friends, it's probably not legit.

246
00:26:39,950 --> 00:26:42,128
So try not to swing from one side to the other.

247
00:26:42,288 --> 00:26:44,928
The truth is usually somewhere in the middle.

248
00:26:45,262 --> 00:26:52,772
And for those of you who are more technical, my friends, I think we have a responsibility to help our non-technical friends see it that way as well.

249
00:26:53,230 --> 00:27:02,436
Fear isn't how this technology gets better, and fear is definitely not going to make a better world for any of us, even if it might get somebody a trillion dollar IPO.

250
00:27:02,926 --> 00:27:09,088
And if you want to know whether AI is actually going to kill us all, I went through a whole stack of research papers on that one as well.

251
00:27:09,422 --> 00:27:11,008
I'm linking it on your screen right now.

252
00:27:11,182 --> 00:27:12,484
That's the video that I would watch next.

